//! MAPPING — one installed protection row, with a handle on it (spec §2.5;
//! sawos design 6 D-1/D-3).
//!
//! **A MAPPING IS AN INSTALLED GRANT ROW.**
//!
//! **AND SINCE M5 UNIT 2 IT HAS A VIRTUAL HALF** (sawos design 33). This header
//! used to say SOS does not translate and that §2.5's "installed virtual
//! placement" therefore had none — true of every tier until arm64 grew
//! per-process page tables, and still true of the tiers that stayed. What a
//! Mapping records is which process's domain carries the row and which row it
//! is; `base()` below is how a program reads the address that row was placed at,
//! and on a tier that does not translate the answer is the region's own address,
//! which is that tier's answer rather than a missing one. §5.5's identity law
//! re-scopes with it — unit 8 owns the spec prose.
//!
//! **ONLY A MAPPING UNMAPS** (§2.5), which is the object's whole reason to
//! exist. That is why `Process.map` answers with one instead of returning a
//! status: without a handle onto the row there would be no way to take it back,
//! and revocation would have to be some other mechanism.
//!
//! It names only the floor, so `sos.system` — which produces one — can name it.

import sosabi.{SosStatus, MappingHandle, MappingOp, NO_HANDLE}

import sos.floor.{call_value, checked, sos_handle_release, sos_mapping_unmap}

/// A handle to a Mapping — one protection row, installed.
///
/// Built by `Process.map` and by nothing else.
///
/// **`NoCopy`, AND DROPPING IT RELEASES THE HANDLE — WHICH IS NOT AN UNMAP.**
/// That distinction is §2.5's ruled stance rather than an implementation
/// detail, and it is worth reading carefully because the RAII habit points the
/// other way. Release destroys the caller's ENTRY and never the object (design
/// 3 D-2's entries-never-touch-objects rule), so a `Mapping` that falls out of
/// scope leaves its row installed: **the mapping is permanent, safe-but-leaked,
/// never a dangling-unmap hazard.** A caller that means to revoke calls
/// `unmap()` and then drops the value.
///
/// §2.5 sketches a Deinit that unmaps at scope exit, so that "the common path
/// is leak-free by construction". SOS does not do that today and the reason is
/// stated rather than hidden: unmapping in a `deinit` would make the ROW's
/// lifetime the wrapper's, and a wrapper drops on every path out of a scope
/// including the ones a launcher takes right after handing a child its memory —
/// so the shape unit 4 exists for (map into a child, then start it) would
/// revoke the mapping before the child ever ran.
///
/// **THE REFCOUNT ARRIVED IN M3 UNIT 5 AND DID NOT CHANGE THAT** (sawos design
/// 7 D-2), which is why this note now records a RULING rather than a wait. A
/// Mapping OWNS its row; the row is not a counted reference back. So a
/// mapped-but-unreferenced Mapping — every handle released, the grant still
/// installed — reaches zero, FREES ITS SLOT AND LEAVES THE ROW, and the row is
/// then unremovable until the TARGET process's teardown. That is exactly the
/// permanent-but-safe stance above, made mechanical instead of promised. An
/// unmapping `deinit` is therefore not blocked on a mechanism any more; it is
/// declined on the launcher argument, and the escape hatch is the one it always
/// was — call `unmap()` where revocation is meant, and let the drop be a drop.
public struct Mapping {
    public(package) handle: MappingHandle,
}

extension Mapping: NoCopy {
    /// Drop is release, and release is NOT unmap — see the type's docstring.
    /// Otherwise this is `System`'s deinit exactly: the sentinel and the
    /// discard for the same reasons.
    func deinit(&var self) {
        if self.handle != NO_HANDLE {
            let _ = sos_handle_release(self.handle)
        }
    }
}

extension Mapping {
    /// **TAKE THE ROW OUT OF THE DOMAIN IT WAS INSTALLED IN.** Requires
    /// `MappingRight.Unmap`.
    ///
    /// The memory becomes unreachable to the target process at once — not at
    /// its next switch, AT ONCE, even when the target is the process running
    /// right now. That is the live-domain rule (design 6 D-3) and it is the
    /// half of this op a caller most needs to be able to rely on: an `unmap`
    /// that returned `Ok` and left the window open until some later reschedule
    /// would be a revocation that did not revoke.
    ///
    /// **UNMAPPING TWICE TERMINATES THE CALLER** (`BadState`), and so does
    /// unmapping a Mapping whose target process has DIED — its whole domain
    /// went with it, so there is no row left to remove. Both are
    /// caller-checkable: the caller either unmapped it or created the child.
    ///
    /// It consumes nothing. The wrapper stays valid and still owns its handle,
    /// which is what makes "unmap, then drop" one ordinary sequence rather than
    /// a consuming funnel with a disarm; there is no word moving between tables
    /// here, only a row leaving a record.
    public func unmap(&self) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        checked(sos_mapping_unmap(self.handle))
    }

    /// **WHERE THIS MAPPING IS.** Requires `MappingRight.Base`.
    ///
    /// **THE ADDRESS IS THE ANSWER, AND THIS IS WHERE IT IS ANSWERED** (sawos
    /// design 33; design 25 ruling 1). A caller supplies no address to `map` on
    /// any tier — a hint is an opinion no tier could honor uniformly — so the
    /// kernel chooses, and a program treats what comes back as DATA. That is
    /// design 19's portable idiom, and until this method existed the tree had no
    /// way to write it: `map` answers a capability, a `Memory` has no bounds
    /// reader, and the address had to come from a build-time constant that only
    /// happened to be right while user addresses were physical ones.
    ///
    /// The number is a USER address in the TARGET's address space — which is the
    /// caller's own when a process maps into itself, and the child's when a
    /// launcher maps into a child. Two processes holding two Mappings of ONE
    /// region may legitimately get two different answers; on a tier that does
    /// not translate they get the same one, and neither is a fact a portable
    /// program should assume.
    ///
    /// Asking an already-unmapped Mapping, or one whose target has died, ends
    /// the caller with `BadState` — `unmap`'s own two refusals, since it is the
    /// same husk in both cases.
    public func base(&self) -> Result<UInt, SosStatus> {
        call_value(self.handle, MappingOp.Base as UInt, 0, 0, 0)
    }
}
